*[Retained footnote]*

1. As Judge Wald pointed out in her _Life Partners_ dissent, [I]f the realization of profits depends significantly on the post-investment operation of market forces, pre-investment activities by a promoter would not satisfy _Howey_\u{2019}s third prong. In such a situation, the realization of investor profits is fundamentally outside of the promoter\u{2019}s control and the investor\u{2019}s dependence on the promoter is more circumscribed. 87 F.3d at 552 (Wald, J., dissenting); see also _Noa v. Key Futures, Inc._, 638 F.2d 77, 79 (9th Cir. 1980) (holding that the _Howey_ test was not met where \u{201C}the profits to the investor depended upon the fluctuations of the silver market, not the managerial efforts of [the promoter]\u{201D}). When profits depend upon market forces, public information is available to investors by which they can independently evaluate the possible success of the investment. In the case before us, investors were far more dependent on the efforts and information provided by MBC than an investor relying on the open market to produce a profit.
]

